# Website deploys (staging and production)

The live website (`reetle.co`) must only ever talk to the production API.
`.env.local` points at the staging API, so a plain `npm run build` produces a
site that talks to staging. Deploying such a build to production sent real
users to the staging database on 2026-09-24.

## The only allowed deploy commands

```bash
npm run deploy:staging      # builds against the staging API, deploys reetle-staging-web
npm run deploy:production   # builds against the production API, deploys reetle.co
```

## Never

- Never run `npm run build` followed by `firebase deploy ... hosting:production`.
- Never run a plain `firebase deploy` (it deploys both sites).
- Never edit or bypass the `predeploy` hooks in `firebase.json` or
  `scripts/check-deploy-target.mjs`, and never remove the `PRODUCTION_HOSTNAMES`
  override in `src/config/environment.ts` or the matching check in the
  `layout.tsx` prefetch script.
- Never deploy to production without the user explicitly asking for a
  production deploy.

## Safeguards in place

1. `src/config/environment.ts` forces the production API on production
   hostnames, whatever the build was configured with.
2. `firebase.json` runs `scripts/check-deploy-target.mjs` before each hosting
   deploy. A production deploy fails if the build contains the staging API URL
   or lacks the production one; a staging deploy fails if it lacks the staging
   API URL.

## After a production deploy

Confirm the live site references the production API and not staging:

```bash
curl -s https://reetle.co/ -o /tmp/rc.html && for js in $(rg -o '/_next/static/[^"]+\.js' /tmp/rc.html | sort -u); do curl -s "https://reetle.co$js"; done > /tmp/rc.js
rg -c 'reetle-api-staging' /tmp/rc.html /tmp/rc.js   # must find nothing
rm -f /tmp/rc.html /tmp/rc.js
```

- Staging permanent URL: `https://reetle-staging-web.web.app`
